   "text": {
    "L1": "Breathing and respiration are connected, but they are not the same thing.",
    "L2": "Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs. Respiration happens in every cell.",
    "L3": "When we breathe in, the chest expands, the diaphragm moves down, and air enters the lungs.",
    "L4": "When we breathe out, the chest returns and air is pushed out.",
    "L5": "In respiration, cells break down glucose, usually using oxygen, to release energy. Carbon dioxide and water are produced.",
    "L6": "Exhaled air still contains mostly nitrogen, but it has less oxygen, more carbon dioxide, and more water vapour than inhaled air.",
    "L7": "During exercise, muscles need more energy, so cells respire faster. We breathe faster and deeper to supply oxygen and remove carbon dioxide.",
    "L8": "Animals use different ways to take in oxygen: fish use gills, earthworms use moist skin, insects use spiracles, and frogs use lungs and skin."
   },
